---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Fabric CLI AI Agent Infrastructure | Jeremy Hoover"
  description="How I identified, designed, and implemented the public AI asset system shipped with the Microsoft Fabric CLI general availability release."
>
  <article class="section case-study case-study--green">
    <header class="case-hero">
      <div class="container container--narrow">
        <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="back">← Back to selected work</a>

        <span class="tag">Open source · Microsoft Fabric CLI · Agent infrastructure</span>
        <h1>Designing the AI execution layer for Fabric CLI</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          I identified a missing product layer before the Fabric CLI general availability release.
          AI assistants could run the CLI, but they lacked a shared system for understanding
          Fabric and completing repeatable tasks reliably. I proposed the AI asset system, shaped
          it with the product manager, and implemented the context, mode, prompt, skills,
          references, and scripts in my public contributions.
        </p>

        <div class="outcomes-strip" aria-label="Project outcomes">
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">March 2026</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">Shipped with Fabric CLI at GA</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">36 files</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">in the primary AI assets pull request</span>
          </div>
          <div class="outcome">
            <span class="outcome__num">Public</span>
            <span class="outcome__label">PRs, release notes, and a co-authored launch article</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="case-body">
      <div class="container case-shell">
        <section class="case-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2 id="summary-title">At a glance</h2>
            <dl class="case-meta">
              <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Senior Content Designer</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Ownership</dt><dd>Initiative, system design, and implementation for my public contributions</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Collaboration</dt><dd>Fabric CLI product manager and engineering team</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Timeframe</dt><dd>December 2025 to March 2026</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Shipped at GA. The public repository continues to evolve.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>Public pull requests, commits, release notes, and GA article</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </section>

        <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="Fabric CLI case study sections">
          <span class="case-toc__label">Jump to a section</span>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#gap">Product gap</a></li>
            <li><a href="#decision">System decision</a></li>
            <li><a href="#implementation">Implementation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#validation">Validation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#response">Response</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reflection">Reflection</a></li>
          </ul>
        </nav>

        <section id="gap" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>The product gap</h2>
          <p>
            The Fabric CLI gave developers a direct way to operate Microsoft Fabric from a
            terminal. As more people connected it to GitHub Copilot, Claude, Cursor, and other
            assistants, a new problem emerged: each agent needed enough product context to use the
            CLI correctly, and users were rebuilding the same instructions for repeatable tasks.
          </p>
          <p>
            There was no single source of truth for this work. Agents needed to understand
            Fabric's hierarchy, command patterns, authentication, safety rules, and verification
            steps. They also needed practical workflows that went beyond a long reference
            document.
          </p>
          <p>I thought the CLI needed a structured agent layer, not another set of disconnected prompts.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="role-title">
          <h2 id="role-title">Expanding my role</h2>
          <p>
            My official role was Senior Content Designer supporting the Fabric CLI product and
            engineering teams. This system was not part of the original release plan. I brought
            the opportunity forward because the boundary between product guidance, interaction
            design, and implementation was changing quickly as agents became real CLI users.
          </p>
          <p>
            I worked with the product manager to define the direction using industry practice,
            our experience with the CLI, and feedback from community power users. I then
            implemented the files in my pull requests and revised them through product and
            engineering review.
          </p>
          <p>
            The work expanded my role from improving individual product experiences to designing
            the infrastructure that shaped how agents interacted with the product.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="decision" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>One file would not solve the problem</h2>
          <p>
            We considered several approaches: a different folder structure, fewer and broader
            skills, no foundational core skill, one universal context file, and an
            <code>AGENTS.md</code>-only model.
          </p>
          <p>
            A universal file looked simpler, but it would mix product context, agent behavior,
            repeatable workflows, and task instructions in one place. It also would not account
            for the different file formats, installation paths, and loading models used by
            Copilot, Claude, and Cursor.
          </p>
          <p>
            We separated the system into four layers instead. The core skill created a shared
            foundation, and specialized skills could build on it without repeating the same
            authentication, hierarchy, safety, and command guidance.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="architecture-caption">
          <div class="architecture" role="img" aria-label="Four connected layers of the Fabric CLI AI asset system: context, modes, prompts, and skills">
            <div class="architecture__item"><span class="architecture__number" aria-hidden="true">01</span><h3>Context</h3><p>Gives each assistant the Fabric and CLI mental model it needs.</p></div>
            <div class="architecture__item"><span class="architecture__number" aria-hidden="true">02</span><h3>Modes</h3><p>Define specialized agent behavior and operating rules.</p></div>
            <div class="architecture__item"><span class="architecture__number" aria-hidden="true">03</span><h3>Prompts</h3><p>Provide reusable entry points for common workflows.</p></div>
            <div class="architecture__item"><span class="architecture__number" aria-hidden="true">04</span><h3>Skills</h3><p>Package task guidance, references, and executable scripts.</p></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption id="architecture-caption">
            <strong>A maintainable agent layer.</strong> The system separated shared product
            context, specialized behavior, repeatable prompts, and task-specific skills instead
            of forcing every tool and workflow into one universal file.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="implementation" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>What I implemented</h2>
          <p>My primary AI assets pull request introduced or reorganized 36 files. I wrote the files included in my contribution, including:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Tool-specific context for GitHub Copilot, Claude, and Cursor</li>
            <li>A custom Fabric CLI agent mode</li>
            <li>A reusable workspace-creation prompt</li>
            <li>Core and Power BI skills</li>
            <li>Detailed command and scenario references</li>
            <li>Automation scripts for health checks, semantic model refresh, refresh history, and report rebinding</li>
            <li>Repository guidance connecting contributors and agents to the right source material</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The repository has continued to evolve since GA. The public pull request preserves
            the exact scope of what I contributed at launch.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
          <h2 id="workflow-title">Designing around real agent workflows</h2>
          <p>
            The final scope was not based only on what we thought agents might need. Community
            members were already describing how they used agents with the CLI, how they got better
            results, and which types of skills supported their workflows.
          </p>
          <p>
            That feedback helped us decide which skills to prioritize, how users would access
            them, and how the system should work across different assistants. We focused the
            release on a core skill, Power BI scenarios, and workspace creation rather than trying
            to cover every possible Fabric task at once.
          </p>
        </section>

        <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-labelledby="flow-caption">
          <ol class="agent-flow">
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">1</span><strong>User goal</strong><span>Create or manage a Fabric resource</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">2</span><strong>Tool context</strong><span>Load the right product and CLI model</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">3</span><strong>Prompt or skill</strong><span>Apply task-specific guidance</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">4</span><strong>CLI action</strong><span>Run concise Fabric commands</span></li>
            <li><span class="agent-flow__step">5</span><strong>Verification</strong><span>Check authentication, paths, and results</span></li>
          </ol>
          <figcaption id="flow-caption">
            <strong>From intent to verified action.</strong> The agent loads the guidance it needs
            for the task instead of relying on a generic prompt or a complete API specification.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <section id="validation" class="content-section">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2>Review, validation, and release</h2>
            <p>
              The product manager reviewed the direction, and the engineering team reviewed and
              tested the implementation. The public pull request also passed the repository's
              automated checks before it was approved and merged.
            </p>
            <p>
              The work shipped as part of the Fabric CLI general availability
              release. The public release notes credit the AI assets, and I co-authored the GA
              article with the product manager to explain why the CLI was becoming an execution
              layer for agents.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="proof-list" aria-label="Public project evidence">
            <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/fabric-cli/pull/176" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Primary contribution</span><strong>Inspect AI assets PR #176</strong><small>36 files, review history, checks, and merged commits ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/fabric-cli/blob/main/.changes/v1.5.0.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Release evidence</span><strong>Read the v1.5 release notes</strong><small>Public contribution credit and release date ↗</small></a>
            <a href="https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-Updates-Blog/Fabric-CLI-v1-5-is-here-Generally-Available/ba-p/5172135" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><span>Product framing</span><strong>Read the co-authored GA article</strong><small>Agent execution model, scenarios, and contributors ↗</small></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="response" class="content-section">
          <div class="reading-column">
            <h2>The public response</h2>
            <p>
              The release gave us qualitative evidence that the product shift was meaningful.
              External users did not describe the agent layer as extra documentation. They
              described it as a change in what the CLI could be.
            </p>
          </div>
          <figure class="response-quote">
            <blockquote>
              “The agent execution layer is the piece I’m most excited about. We’ve been building
              agent-driven Fabric workflows at DataMartIn, and a CLI purpose-built for assistants
              to operate it fluently changes everything.”
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption><strong>Martin Rojze</strong><span>Public response to the Fabric CLI GA announcement</span></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="reading-column">
            <p>
              Leon Gordon made a similar distinction, describing the work as a shift from a
              developer tool to an agent surface. These responses do not provide an adoption count,
              but they show that users understood the system and connected it to real workflows.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="reading-column content-section" aria-labelledby="next-title">
          <h2 id="next-title">What happened next</h2>
          <p>The GA work helped move product and engineering conversations toward a broader question: which repeatable workflows should become skills next?</p>
          <p>
            After the public release, I created internal skills for engineering teams I
            supported. That work built on the same principle, but it solved different
            team-specific problems and needs its own evidence and story. I keep it separate from
            the public Fabric CLI contribution.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="reflection" class="reading-column content-section">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>
          <p>
            The important shift was not teaching an agent a list of commands. It was designing a
            maintainable system that connected product knowledge, task guidance, tool-specific
            setup, and verification.
          </p>
          <p>
            I think this project also shows how design roles are changing. The work required
            product judgment, information architecture, interaction design, technical writing,
            repository implementation, and close collaboration with engineering. The title
            mattered less than identifying the missing system and taking responsibility for
            getting it into the product.
          </p>
        </section>

        <aside class="inspect-work" aria-labelledby="inspect-title">
          <div>
            <span class="tag">Public and inspectable</span>
            <h2 id="inspect-title">Inspect the work</h2>
            <p>The pull request, release notes, launch article, and current repository show the system, review process, release context, and how the work evolved.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="inspect-work__actions">
            <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/fabric-cli/pull/176" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn">View AI assets PR ↗</a>
            <a href="https://community.fabric.microsoft.com/t5/Fabric-Updates-Blog/Fabric-CLI-v1-5-is-here-Generally-Available/ba-p/5172135" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn--outline">Read GA article ↗</a>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <hr class="divider" />
        <div class="nav-row">
          <a href={`${base}/work/org-ai-initiatives`} class="btn btn--outline">← Previous: Org AI initiatives</a>
          <a href={`${base}/#projects`} class="btn">All selected projects</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-study { padding-top: 0; }
  .tag { display: inline-block; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  h1 { margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-6); }
  .lede { max-width: 58ch; margin-bottom: var(--space-12); font-size: var(--size-xl); line-height: 1.6; }
  .outcome { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .outcome:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .outcome:first-child .outcome__num { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 13cqw, 2rem); white-space: nowrap; }
  .outcome__label { max-width: 24ch; font-size: var(--size-sm); color: var(--muted); }
  .case-shell { max-width: calc(var(--narrow) + 6rem); }
  .reading-column { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin-inline: auto; }
  .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 90px; }
  .content-section h2, .case-summary h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .content-section:not(:first-child) h2 { margin-top: var(--space-16); }
  .content-section p { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .content-section ul { margin: var(--space-4) 0 var(--space-6) var(--space-6); }
  .content-section li { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
  code { padding: 0.15em 0.4em; border-radius: 3px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-family: var(--f-mono); font-size: 0.875em; }
  .case-meta { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); }
  .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-left: 4px solid var(--portfolio-blue-soft); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .case-meta div:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .case-meta dt { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-meta dd { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .case-toc { max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto var(--space-16); padding-block: var(--space-6); border-block: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .case-toc__label { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 0.6875rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.1em; line-height: 1.2; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .case-toc ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem 0; margin: 0; list-style: none; }
  .case-toc li { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-coral); display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .case-toc li + li { margin-left: var(--space-4); }
  .case-toc li + li::before { content: ''; width: 2px; height: 16px; margin-right: var(--space-4); border-radius: 999px; background: var(--toc-divider); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .case-toc li:nth-child(3n + 1) { --toc-divider: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .case-toc a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 32px; align-items: center; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 650; line-height: 1.3; text-decoration: none; }
  .case-toc a:hover { color: var(--portfolio-blue); text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-color: var(--portfolio-coral); text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-underline-offset: 5px; }
  .evidence-figure { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-12) auto var(--space-16); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption { width: 100%; max-width: none; margin-top: var(--space-4); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .evidence-figure figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }
  .architecture { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #b8c9ee; border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue-soft); }
  .architecture__item { position: relative; min-height: 210px; padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid rgba(16,26,45,0.1); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .architecture__item:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .architecture__item::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -1.05rem; z-index: 1; display: grid; width: 1.55rem; height: 1.55rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .architecture__item:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .architecture__number { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--space-8); color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-family: var(--f-heading); font-size: 0.8rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
  .architecture h3 { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); color: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .architecture p { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.875rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .agent-flow { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: var(--space-2); margin: 0; padding: var(--space-4); border: 1px solid #e1c35f; border-radius: 20px; background: #fff8da; list-style: none; }
  .agent-flow li { position: relative; display: flex; min-height: 170px; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-2); padding: var(--space-4); border-radius: 12px; background: #fff; color: var(--muted); transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .agent-flow li:hover { z-index: 2; transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .agent-flow li::after { content: '→'; position: absolute; top: 50%; right: -0.75rem; z-index: 1; color: var(--portfolio-blue); font-size: 1.25rem; font-weight: 800; transform: translateY(-50%); }
  .agent-flow li:last-child::after { content: none; }
  .agent-flow__step { display: grid; width: 1.75rem; height: 1.75rem; place-items: center; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--portfolio-blue); color: #fff; font-size: 0.75rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .agent-flow strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .agent-flow li > span:last-child { font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.45; }
  .proof-list { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-3); margin: var(--space-8) auto 0; }
  .proof-list a { display: flex; min-height: 176px; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); padding: var(--space-6); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 14px; background: #fff; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .proof-list a:hover { text-decoration: none; transform: translateY(-4px); box-shadow: 0 12px 28px rgba(16,26,45,0.12); }
  .proof-list span { color: var(--portfolio-coral); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 800; letter-spacing: 0.07em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .proof-list strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .proof-list small { margin-top: auto; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .response-quote { width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); margin: var(--space-8) auto; }
  .response-quote blockquote { margin: 0; padding: var(--space-8); border-left: 5px solid var(--portfolio-coral); border-radius: 0 16px 16px 0; background: #fff; color: var(--portfolio-ink); font-family: var(--f-heading); font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 2.5vw, 1.5rem); font-style: normal; line-height: 1.5; transition: transform 180ms ease, box-shadow 180ms ease; }
  .response-quote blockquote:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); box-shadow: 0 10px 24px rgba(16,26,45,0.1); }
  .response-quote figcaption { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.1rem; padding: var(--space-3) 0 0 var(--space-8); color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--size-sm); }
  .response-quote figcaption strong { color: var(--portfolio-ink); }
  .inspect-work { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.4fr) minmax(220px, 0.6fr); width: 100%; max-width: var(--narrow); gap: var(--space-8); align-items: center; margin: var(--space-16) auto 0; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 3rem); border-radius: 20px; background: var(--portfolio-blue); }
  .inspect-work .tag { color: var(--portfolio-yellow); }
  .inspect-work h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); color: #fff; }
  .inspect-work p { color: rgba(255,255,255,0.82); }
  .inspect-work__actions { display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .inspect-work .btn { display: flex; min-height: 48px; align-items: center; justify-content: center; background: var(--portfolio-yellow); color: var(--portfolio-ink); text-align: center; }
  .inspect-work .btn:hover { background: #ffe28a; }
  .inspect-work .btn--outline { border-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); background: transparent; color: #fff; }
  .inspect-work .btn--outline:hover { border-color: #fff; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); }
  .nav-row { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-4); }
  .divider { margin-block: var(--space-16) var(--space-8); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .case-toc ul { gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem; }
    .case-toc li + li { margin-left: 0; }
    .case-toc li + li::before { content: none; }
    .architecture { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .architecture__item { min-height: 180px; }
    .architecture__item::after { content: none; }
    .agent-flow { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .agent-flow li { min-height: 145px; }
    .agent-flow li::after { content: none; }
    .proof-list { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .proof-list a { min-height: 0; }
    .proof-list small { margin-top: 0; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .content-section { scroll-margin-top: 130px; }
    .case-meta { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .case-meta div { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc { margin-top: var(--space-6); margin-bottom: var(--space-12); padding-block: var(--space-4); }
    .case-toc ul { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); width: 100%; gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-6); }
    .case-toc a { min-height: 44px; }
    .architecture { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
    .architecture__item { min-height: auto; }
    .architecture__number { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
    .agent-flow { grid-template-columns: 1fr; padding: var(--space-3); }
